  "description": {
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Juglans microcarpa",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juglans_microcarpa",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 1373722070,
      "text": "Juglans microcarpa, known also as the little walnut, Texas walnut, Texas black walnut or little black walnut, is a large shrub or small tree which grows wild along streams and ravines in Texas, New Mexico, Oklahoma, and Kansas, and the northernmost states of Mexico. It produces nuts with a width of 1/2—3/4 in. The pinnately compound leaves bear 7—25 untoothed to finely-toothed leaflets, each 1/4—1/2 in wide. It is found at elevations ranging from 700 ft to 6700 ft."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Juglans microcarpa",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juglans_microcarpa",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 238534912,
      "text": "Juglans microcarpa, le noyer du Texas, est une espèce d'arbres de la famille des Juglandaceae. C'est un noyer de petite taille, d'une dizaine de mètres de haut, son feuillage composé a des folioles plus petit que le noyer commun, sa noix fait environ 1 cm de diamètre."
    }
